@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: where a credential is allowed to exist, who injects it, and what the agent may hold
' covers: internal/credentials/**
' covers: internal/provider/**
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go (Spec: secrets, forwards)
' covers: cmd/proveo/main.go
' covers: cmd/proveo/wizard.go
' covers: internal/run/run.go
' covers: internal/run/helpers.go (renamedEnv, StateDir)
' covers: internal/run/env_names_test.go
' covers: e2e/claudecode_auth_test.go
' covers: e2e/opencode_auth_test.go
' covers: e2e/credentials_test.go
' covers: defs/cecli/harness.manifest
' covers: internal/contract/cecli_providers_test.go
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/provider_gate_test.go
'   who brokers on sbx, and the forward flag . _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml
'   the .env guard inside sbx ................ _spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml
'
' ── DECIDED 2026-10-16: THE BOUNDARY IS sbx's ──────────────────────────────
' The Executor: "Retire Docker egress"; "Refuse without sbx". proveo's own broker
' is DELETED with the docker backend: internal/broker (inject on route, strip off
' it), cmd/proveo-egress, the "proveo-brokered" sentinel and
' PROVEO_CREDENTIAL_BROKER_KEYS (apply_broker_sentinel, ApplyBrokerSentinel,
' DefaultSentinel), <stateDir>/inject/broker.env and WriteBrokerEnv. MEASURED:
' none of them is in the tree.
'
' What holds the boundary now, MEASURED in internal/backend/sandbox Spec:
'   broker   every declared secret and every capability-permitted provider key
'            goes to `sbx secret set --force` on stdin; sbx's proxy attaches it
'            at egress. The agent holds sbx's own placeholder, never the value.
'   forward  the same names travel as a bare `-e NAME`; sbx copies the value
'            from proveo's child environment (credentials.ChildEnv). The agent
'            holds the real value.
'   neither  a credential the proxy cannot attach (StoreUninjectable: a signing
'            key, a credentials file) is forwarded on either route.
' secret-custody (d) — a plaintext credential in a mounted file — is CLOSED: no
' proveo proxy needs a key, so broker.env has no reader and is not written.
'
' What the deleted broker did that sbx does NOT do, kept so nobody assumes it:
' it STRIPPED authorization / x-api-key / x-goog-api-key / api-key /
' proxy-authorization off every request bound for a non-provider host, and its
' DLP scan blocked a credential-shaped body. [Unverified] whether sbx strips an
' agent-supplied header off-route; nothing in this tree asserts it.
'
' ── AN EMPTY `capabilities.providers` IS NOT A WIDE CAPABILITY ──────────────
' MEASURED 2026-09-08, from a live cecli run's own consent screen: the kit
' declared a CURSOR credential. cecli can never spend one — Cursor has no
' bring-your-own-key path — and it was declared because
' `manifest.listAllows` treats an EMPTY providers list as "allow every
' provider". That is the ABSENCE of a capability statement being read as the
' widest possible one, and the two are not the same thing.
'
' The cost is not only a stray declaration. `proxyManaged` sets the named
' variable to a SENTINEL inside the sandbox, so every undeclared-but-detected
' provider becomes a non-empty key in the agent's environment. A harness that
' reads os.environ to decide which providers to talk to — cecli's
' ModelProviderManager does exactly this, skipping a provider silently when no
' key is set — then contacts a provider nobody chose. `/model` probing
' `api.x.ai/v1/models` was proveo handing cecli a key, not a network fault.
'
' THE FIX IS A DERIVED LIST, WRITTEN OUT (defs/cecli/harness.manifest; moved
' from its header, N14). cecli's providers are proveo's registry
' (internal/provider/provider.go) intersected with what cecli can route to —
' the union of cecli/resources/providers.json (63 OpenAI-compatible gateway
' slugs), cecli/helpers/llms/providers/* (its per-provider adapters) and
' litellm's native providers. That intersection is every brokered provider
' EXCEPT cursor. It is spelled out rather than written as an exclusion because
' the manifest schema has no negative form; internal/contract
' cecli_providers_test.go fails when the registry grows and the list does not,
' so the drift is loud rather than silent.
'
' THE GATE IS NOW LOCAL RATHER THAN INHERITED. sandbox.Spec re-checked
' AllowsProvider for credentials (agentCredentials) and for env-var forwarding,
' but built the Kit allowlist straight from `in.Detected` and trusted its caller
' to have filtered. run.go:433 does filter, so nothing was wrong in production —
' but a forbidden provider KEPT ITS REACH while losing its credential, and an
' invariant that holds only because one call site remembers is not an invariant.
' All three paths applied the same filter. Since 2026-10-16 the Kit allowlist
' carries no provider reach at all (_spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline.puml), so
' the third path is gone and the filter guards the secret and forward loops.
' covers: e2e/sbx_test.go
title Credential boundary — sbx's store holds the value; the agent holds a placeholder (broker) or the value (forward)
'
' ── A GATEWAY WHOSE /v1/models IS PUBLIC NEEDS A DIFFERENT PROBE ───────────
' The anthropic assertion works because GET /v1/models is authenticated and
' costs nothing. OpenCode's own gateway is not: it answers 200 with no header
' and 200 with a bad one (probed 2026-09-01), so it cannot tell a delivered
' credential from a dropped one.
'
' The cheapest request that CAN is a one-token chat completion on the plan's
' least expensive PAID model — 401 without a valid key, 200 with one. On Zen
' that spends a fraction of a cent; on a Go subscription nothing extra. A FREE
' model would not do: opencode.ai serves free models to an unauthenticated
' caller, so the probe would pass with the boundary broken.
'
' Each plan is its own case and each skips unless the HOST itself gets 200 on
' that plan's endpoint — a Zen key is not a Go key, and asking host-side first
' is what lets a container failure be attributed to the egress layer rather
' than to the key.
'
' Under `broker` the container holds only sbx's placeholder and sends it in the
' auth header; sbx's proxy SUBSTITUTES the real key on the way out. The probe
' sends the header in both modes for that reason: omitting it left sbx nothing
' to substitute, and the 401 read as a failed credential (e2e/opencode_auth_test
' .go opencodeProbeLine). HISTORY: proveo's deleted MITM REPLACED whatever
' header arrived, so on docker an unauthenticated request answering 200 was the
' brokering assertion. On this endpoint neither can pass by accident.
'
' `forward` used to be skipped throughout, because opencode declared no secret
' in its manifest env and so had nothing to forward — the key could reach a
' forward run only through a workspace .env the entrypoint autoloaded (it no
' longer does inside sbx), which an empty probe workspace does not have. The
' def declares OPENCODE_API_KEY now
' (_spec/defs/opencode/opencode-paradigm.puml), so that case RUNS: the test
' reads the declared set out of the def rather than restating it, which is what
' lets a skip stop being a skip the moment the declaration lands.
'
' From 2026-09-06 OpenCode also REQUIRES an `x-opencode-session` header:
' upstream mailed to say requests without it "may error", naming the two
' user-agents at fault — "Go HTTP client" and "curl", which are precisely the
' host-side precondition and the in-container assertion. The value is ONE stable
' id per conversation, so each plan gets its own, stable for the life of the
' process: the two halves of a case ask the same question of the same endpoint
' and must not look like two conversations, while zen and go are separate.
' The plans and their cheapest paid model, from models.dev on 2026-09-01:
' zen https://opencode.ai/zen/v1 deepseek-v4-flash, go
' https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1 glm-5.3-flash. A retired model skips with the
' status host-side rather than failing the boundary.
'
' The probe runs beside the agent rather than through it: `proveo run opencode`
' launches through its kit, and awaitKit + kitRun.exec run curl in the sandbox
' through `sbx exec` (MEASURED, e2e/opencode_auth_test.go). It used `--shell`
' until that flag was retired 2026-10-17. The topology is identical and the
' credential question is answerable with curl. Its env file
' holds ONLY the one credential, so the broker pins opencode rather than
' refusing over a multi-key host, and PROVEO_HOME is isolated so a developer's
' own mounted home can neither supply nor suppress anything.
' The claudecode case's env file (writeSingleCredentialEnv) holds ONE
' credential too. It once also forwarded the role names, so the harness would
' land on the provider that key pays for. proveo no longer chooses a model
' (_spec/internal/entrypoint/model-alias-bridges.puml), so they would steer
' nothing and only copy the operator's shell into a test fixture.
'
' MORE THINGS THE PROBE HAS TO PIN, each of which produced a green run
' that measured nothing:
'   HISTORY: PROVEO_SBX=off was once pinned here, to reach proveo's own egress
'     layer. That layer is deleted and the value now refuses the run; the
'     probe runs on sbx with no pin.
'   The secret never travels on argv. The auth header is written
'     double-quoted so the CONTAINER's shell expands it out of its own
'     environment, and the response BODY never reaches the pane. `--max-time`
'     keeps a blocked request from hanging the shell instead of failing it.
'
' ── THE BODY IS NOW READ, AND STILL NEVER PRINTED ──────────────────────────
' `-o /dev/null` discarded the one thing that separates a broken boundary from
' an empty wallet. A VALID key on an unfunded account does not answer 401: it
' answers with a body saying "credit balance is too low", and the status alone
' reported that as the egress layer having changed the answer — a red suite for
' a billing state (see MEMORY: the Anthropic key is valid, the wallet empty).
' The body is written to a file INSIDE the sandbox, grepped for that one phrase,
' and deleted in the same command line; only the marker `PROBE_REASON=credits`
' is ever echoed. So the pane still never carries a credential or a completion,
' and the case SKIPS — naming the variable, the mode and the credential posture,
' and saying funding the account is what would exercise the path. A skip for a
' reason proveo does not control is the honest verdict; asserting 200 against an
' account that cannot answer 200 measures the account, not the boundary.
'   The shell echoes the command before running it, so the line still carrying
'     the `%{http_code}` format string is skipped when reading the status back —
'     otherwise the echo is mistaken for a result and the probe passes without
'     ever having reached the provider.

' ── HISTORY: proveo's OWN BROKER, DELETED 2026-10-16 ───────────────────────
' Kept as a record of what the docker path guaranteed, so nobody re-derives it
' or assumes sbx gives the same. All of it lived in internal/broker and
' cmd/proveo-egress (deleted):
'   * the agent held the "proveo-brokered" SENTINEL, named in
'     PROVEO_CREDENTIAL_BROKER_KEYS; the real key sat in <stateDir>/inject/
'     broker.env (0600, outside every agent mount), read once by the MITM.
'   * one ROUTE per provider, matched by destination host (dot-anchored, port
'     included, first match wins); on-route it injected the key (in headers or
'     a query parameter), off-route it STRIPPED authorization, x-api-key,
'     x-goog-api-key, api-key and proxy-authorization whenever any route was
'     configured — the strip was the floor, not the optional half.
'   * a DLP scan rejected credential-shaped bodies; under `forward` it needed
'     PROVEO_EGRESS_PROVIDER_HOSTS (detected ∪ declared providers) so the key's
'     own vendor was not the one host denied.
'   * a vendor-pinned manifest (`provider:`) brokered only that vendor. That
'     key and Manifest.Provider are deleted too; `capabilities.providers` is
'     the only vendor pin left.
' The principle — "inject keys, never expose", borrowed from omnigent's
' credential_proxy — survives; the party enforcing it is sbx.

participant "cmd/proveo run" as RUN <<app>>
participant "internal/provider\nDetect · Resolve" as PROV <<app>>
database "sbx secret store\nHOST-WIDE, outlives the run" as STORE <<data>>
participant "sbx proxy\nthe only route out" as PROXY <<cloud>>
participant "agent process" as AGENT <<app>>
participant "the credential's service host" as VENDOR <<cloud>>
participant "any other host" as OTHER <<cloud>>

RUN ARROW_MAIN PROV : detect keys, filtered by\nthe manifest's providers
RUN ARROW_MAIN STORE : broker: ""sbx secret set --force NAME"" (stdin)
RUN ARROW_ERROR AGENT : forward: bare ""-e NAME"" — the real value
AGENT ARROW_MAIN PROXY : request carrying sbx's placeholder
STORE ARROW_QUEUE PROXY : proxy-managed

alt destination matches a stored service
  PROXY ARROW_CLOUD VENDOR : successLabel(substitute the real key)
else any other destination
  PROXY ARROW_ERROR OTHER : errorLabel(no credential attached)
end

note over PROXY
  **sbx substitutes; it does not strip.**
  The real key replaces sbx's placeholder only
  for a stored service's host. [Unverified]
  whether an agent-held header is stripped
  off-route — proveo's strip went with its MITM.
end note

note over AGENT
  **The mounted "".env"" is readable, never loaded.**
  The image skips sourcing it inside sbx
  (""SANDBOX_NAME""/""SANDBOX_VM_ID""), so a key in it is a FILE the
  agent can read, not an env var it holds.
end note

note over AGENT, VENDOR
  **""forward"" on sbx carries every name broker would store.** Both
  loops in ""sandbox.Spec"" — declared secrets and capability-permitted
  ""provider\.KeyVars()"" — go through ""addSecret"", which forwards under
  ""--credentials forward"". //HISTORY: on docker the KeyVars loop ran only
  under broker, so an undeclared key never reached a forward run.//
  ""e2e/claudecode_auth_test\.go"" still skips forward for an undeclared key.
  [Inference] On sbx that skip is now more conservative than the code.
end note

note over PROXY
  **The boundary is testable without buying a completion.** ""GET
  /v1/models"" is authenticated, returns the account's model list, and
  consumes no tokens — so the crossing can be asserted on any //valid//
  credential rather than only a funded one. Driving a real completion made
  an empty credit balance indistinguishable from a broken broker: both
  surfaced as "no file on the host" after the full eight-minute budget, and
  the pane blamed auth for a billing problem.

  The same request is issued twice, host-side as a precondition and again
  from inside the container. That pairing is the whole diagnostic: a host
  200 with a container non-200 leaves the egress layer as the only thing
  that changed the answer.
end note

note over PROXY
  **An empty wallet is not a broken boundary.**
  A valid key on an unfunded account answers
  with a credit message rather than a refusal,
  so the probe reads the body for that phrase
  and skips instead of blaming the egress layer.
end note

note over RUN
  **Declared-but-missing env is decided before detection, and the order
  is the point.** A key typed at the prompt has to be in hand before
  providers are detected, or it reaches neither sbx's store nor the
  forwarding decision.

  | harness kind | missing declared env |
  | ""subscription: true"" | warn and run anyway — the agent performs its own login, so an ahead-of-time key prompt asks for a credential the harness never uses |
  | anything else, on a TTY | prompt (the env wizard: detect, env short-circuit, TTY only, safe default), then re-check what is still missing |
  | anything else, no TTY | warn only, naming the variable and its description |

  **The subscription harness's login hint is printed before launch, not
  after.** The variables that were missing at that point are kept aside and
  the hints emitted just before the agent starts — a hint about how to
  authenticate is useless once the operator is already inside a session
  running on the other credential.
end note

note over RUN
  **A keyless role is named before launch, not after the call fails.**
  ""roles\.MissingKeys"" warns per role with both halves of the
  diagnosis — ""ARCHITECT_MODEL=<model> needs <VAR> (<provider>), which
  is not set"". Pointing a role at a provider with no key used to
  surface as the harness's own //"invalid API key"//, which says
  nothing about which of three models was at fault.
end note

note over PROV
  **Where a key is looked up, and why the order is fixed.** The lookup
  prefers the process environment, then one host-side ""KEY=VALUE""
  file: an explicit ""PROVEO_ENV_FILE"" wins, else the project
  "".env"" resolved from the invocation dir, the input dir, then the
  repo root (""workspace.ProjectEnvFile""). The lookup reads that file
  **host-side**; no image sources it (N7, 2026-10-17), though the agent
  can read it if it sits in the mounted workspace. It is resolved
  //before// the missing-env prompts, so a key that exists only in a
  project "".env"" is already visible instead of being prompted for twice.
  (F17, 2026-10-17, MEASURED run.go:144 and helpers.go:87-94: the old
  ""PROVEO_EGRESS_ENV_FILE"" is still read when the new name is unset,
  with one warning "… is deprecated and goes away next release — set
  PROVEO_ENV_FILE".)

  **A bare ""-e NAME"" forwards only the client process environment**
  (docker's then, sbx's now), so a secret that lives in that file — or behind
  a ""keychain:"" reference — has to reach the ""sbx"" process itself.
  ""credentials.ChildEnv"" carries it there on ""cmd.Env"" and nowhere
  else. It used to be copied into ""proveo""'s OWN environ with
  ""os.Setenv"", where ""/proc/<pid>/environ"" could read it and every
  later child inherited it; see
  ""_spec/internal/secretref/secret-references\.puml"".
end note

note over PROV
  **Detection is filtered by what the harness declares it can use.**
  ""claudecode"" declares ""providers:[anthropic]"", so listing
  ""OPENAI_API_KEY"" / ""XAI_API_KEY"" / ""GEMINI_API_KEY"" beside it
  implied it could reach them. The auth row already filtered on
  capabilities; the loaded-secrets header line did not, and that is the
  line the operator reads at the confirmation prompt.
end note

note over PROV
  **A provider that accepts more than one credential must not have the
  choice made by declaration order.** ""anthropic"" takes an API key
  //or// a subscription token; whichever the manifest happened to list
  first would otherwise silently win. The ""auth"" row is offered only
  when the operator actually holds more than one of them — a choice is
  shown where one exists, never as decoration.
end note

note over PROV
  **A login must be nameable, or it cannot win.**
  The row listed variables only, so a remembered
  answer naming one outranked a login made later,
  and proveo forwarded a token the API refused
  while a working subscription sat mounted, unread
end note

note over RUN
  **Suppression has to be consulted EVERYWHERE a
  variable can be set, not only where the manifest
  declares one.** There were two loops, and the
  second undid the first.

  The declared-env loop honoured the suppressor.
  The loop over ""provider\.KeyVars()"" did not, and
  its "already added?" guard only skipped names the
  first loop had ACCEPTED — so a variable the first
  loop DECLINED fell through and was given a
  sentinel anyway (proveo's docker sentinel, deleted
  2026-10-16; on sbx the same slot would hold sbx's
  placeholder). Both loops in ""sandbox.Spec"" now
  consult the suppressor (MEASURED).

  A placeholder in that slot is not harmless. The
  agent reads a SET variable as a chosen credential
  whatever it holds, so it displaces the login on
  disk, and a subscription run authenticates as the
  API — the exact misbilling this boundary exists
  to prevent.

  The order it restores: a STORED or MOUNTED
  credential outranks an ambient "".env"" value.
  ""e2e/home_test\.go"" caught it and had been red;
  ""TestAMountedLoginOutranksAnAmbientEnvValue"" pins
  it a layer down, where an e2e run is not needed
  to see it.
end note

note over AGENT
  **A login on disk is a credential that wins —**
  //while it can still authenticate.//
  Where the mounted home holds one, proveo injects
  no auth variable for that provider: an env token
  supersedes the file instead of joining it, which
  billed a subscription run as API usage.

  The qualifier is the whole risk: suppressing a
  working token in favour of a DEAD file leaves the
  run with no credential of any kind.
end note

note over AGENT
  **A file is not a login. A TOKEN is.**
  ""loginUsable"" judged the two expiry stamps only,
  so a record whose tokens had been taken away read
  as healthy-but-stale — usable, needing a refresh
  that had nothing left to refresh with.

  **That is the ordinary state of the proveo home on
  macOS.** Running ""claude"" there moves the credential
  into the **Keychain** — which is machine-global, not
  per-""HOME"" — and rewrites ""\.credentials\.json"" with
  ""accessToken"" and ""refreshToken"" blanked while every
  stamp stays in place. Upstream calls this working as
  intended (anthropics/claude-code#10039, closed as not
  planned), so proveo must recognise the shape, not
  wait for it to stop happening.

  So the check reads the tokens for **emptiness**, never
  for value, and distinguishes ABSENT from BLANK: a field
  that was never there is a shape we do not judge, an
  explicit """" is a token that was removed. A blanked
  refresh token also voids the refresh branch — the stamp
  then describes a renewal that cannot happen.
end note

note over AGENT
  **Reading that file correctly is not the same as reading
  the right file.** ""hasPersistedLogin"" looks under the
  proveo home, and on the **sbx backend nothing does**: sbx
  runs its own agent user and its credential proxy writes
  the credential into THAT user's home from ""sbx secret"".
  The proveo home's copy is not consulted by the run
  — see ""_spec/internal/proveohome/proveo-home-lifecycle\.puml"",
  where moving ""HOME"" onto it orphaned the live credential.

  So on macOS both halves were right and the answer was
  wrong: the file is a husk, ""tokenCleared"" says "no login",
  and proveo REFUSED the run — "needs a subscription login
  and the sbx backend cannot complete one" — while
  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN (stored)"" and
  ""anthropic (oauth configured)"" sat in the store the whole
  time. Every new shell was told to export a token it did
  not need.

  ""sbxStoredAuth"" adds the second store, gated on actually
  reaching that backend. What it learns is **weaker** and is
  said as such: ""sbx secret ls"" prints a name and "(stored)",
  so an entry holding "" "" looks exactly like a live token.
  Presence decides whether to LAUNCH; it never licenses a
  claim about the value. ""sbx\.Available"" is the probe rather
  than ""ensureSbx"" — this runs before the backend is chosen
  and must install nothing and ask nothing.
end note

note over AGENT
  **One suppression predicate.** It governed both the
  docker argv and the sbx Kit while two renderings
  existed (docker deleted 2026-10-16); a credential
  resolved differently per rendering revives the
  divergence a single host-side decision exists to end.

  **HISTORY — the predicate agreed; the rendering did not.** docker
  omitted a suppressed variable, sbx stated it as
  ""-e VAR="" — on the theory that sbx's global secret
  store would otherwise inject a stale value in front of
  the mounted login.

  //Probed and false.// With ""ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"" and
  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"" both in sbx v0\.39\.0's global
  store and no ""-e"" flag, both arrive **UNSET**: sbx
  attaches service secrets as proxy headers and writes the
  harness credential to ""~/\.claude/\.credentials\.json"".
  It never exports them as environment variables, so there
  was nothing in front of the login to override.

  **The empty value was the failure, not the guard.** An
  agent reads a SET variable as a chosen credential whatever
  it holds, and claudecode ranks both of these ABOVE the
  login on disk — so a blank one took the slot the login
  needed, and an unattended run stalled at a prompt asking
  it to approve a key that authenticates nothing. Both
  renderings now omit.
end note

note over AGENT
  **The mounted-"".env"" warning fires on every run whose workspace .env
  holds a provider key.** ""credentials.WarnMountedSecrets(dir, lookup)"",
  sbx-only since 2026-10-16: the agent can READ the file even though the
  image never loads it into the agent's environment (""SANDBOX_NAME""/
  ""SANDBOX_VM_ID"" guard), and nothing proveo runs strips or scans what leaves.

  //HISTORY: it was tier-bound.// It fired on ""open"" only, because the
  intercepting tiers' DLP and header-strip blocked the exfil; and it was dead
  for one release cycle when a tier rename swept ""open"" into the skip list.
  The tiers, the DLP and the strip are gone, so the tier test went with them.
end note

note over AGENT
  **The ""gh"" config directory is a deliberate exception to keeping host
  credentials out of the container.** ""ghConfigMount"" mounts
  ""$GH_CONFIG_DIR"" (else ""~/\.config/gh"") so ""gh"" works inside the
  agent, and ""hosts\.yml"" there holds an OAuth token the agent can read.

  The mount is **read-only**, and read-only prevents //rewriting// the
  host's credential, not //reading// it. What bounds the damage is the
  sandbox boundary and sbx's network policy — not the mount flag.

  The intent is reuse rather than issuance: ""gh"" inside the container picks
  up the session the operator already holds instead of asking for a token of
  its own.

  Opt out with ""PROVEO_MOUNT_GH_CONFIG=0"".
end note

note over RUN
  **What crosses into the sandbox, per declared variable** (MEASURED,
  ""sandbox.Spec""). A non-secret travels as ""NAME=value"" in the Kit
  environment. A secret declared in ""env:"", and every capability-permitted
  provider key, goes to sbx's store under broker or travels as a bare
  ""-e NAME"" under forward; an uninjectable one is forwarded either way.

  //HISTORY: on ""docker run""// a declared secret was replaced by the
  ""proveo-brokered"" sentinel and named in ""PROVEO_CREDENTIAL_BROKER_KEYS"",
  and every undeclared provider key was sentinel-injected the same way. The
  sentinel and its marker variable were deleted 2026-10-16.
end note
@enduml
